# Lever 4's one counter read: is gemm_iq2xxs DRAM-bound in amplified bytes (the slot-tile
# re-read of its weight slabs) or L1TEX/instruction-bound at near-once bytes? The arm is Bolt's
# routed up stack at its P=4096 prefill shape — 432 experts x 2560 rows of 1024 IQ2_XXS values,
# top-9 over a 448-row pass (n_slots 4032), the biggest gemm_iq2xxs launch nsys s1927 saw
# (grid 9900 = 20 row tiles x 495 slot tiles, ~1.34 ms on the A6000).
#
# Paper decomposition [derived, perf-lever4.md]: weight bytes once 292.5 MB (432x2560x66x4),
# activation ~8 MB, output 41.3 MB -> 342 MB; at the measured ~210 GB/s effective the launch is
# ~1.39 ms. The kernel reads each weight slab (14.6 MB > the A6000's 6 MB L2) once per slot tile
# through L1: if those re-reads miss to DRAM, bytes/launch rises toward pin x time (~0.9..1.0 GB
# ceiling); if L2 holds them, DRAM stays ~342 MB and the bind is the per-warp scattered word
# loads (L1TEX wavefronts) or the grid lookups.
#
# Runner (from /Users/hckim/repo/bloomery-l4ncu):
# BLOOMERY_REMOTE='~/repo/bloomery-l4ncu' BLOOMERY_BOX_QUEUE=1 BLOOMERY_BOX_ENV='BLOOMERY_LEASE_CARD=docs/cards/l4-iq2xxs-ncu.card' \
#   just ncu-gpu-gemm gemm_iq2xxs_bolt_up_t448
# Wall [derived]: build ~5 min (cold tree) + the arm's bursts under ncu ~3..5 min.
kind: profile
question: Is gemm_iq2xxs at Bolt's routed-up shape DRAM-bound on amplified weight re-reads (bytes/launch well over the once-set) or bound on L1TEX/instruction work at near-once bytes?
term: dram bytes per profiled launch (MemoryWorkloadAnalysis) against the once-set 342 MB (weights 292.5 + activations ~8 + output 41.3), with the SOL split (DRAM vs L1TEX vs SM) and the top warp-stall reasons of the same launches
unit: GB per launch (and the ratio to the 342 MB once-set)
predict: 0.35..1.0
condition: every profiled launch is the arm's alone (--bench-arm gemm_iq2xxs_bolt_up_t448, grid 9900 x 256 threads), the warm-up burst skipped (BLOOMERY_NCU_SKIP=64), the A6000 under the machine lease with no other compute, sections SpeedOfLight + MemoryWorkloadAnalysis + WarpStateStats
decide-in: ratio ~1 with L1TEX the top unit — the lever is candidate 2 (smem-staged weight words, the Q4_K path) priced against the L1TEX margin; write the round spec on bolt-lever4-iq2 once #328 is on main
decide-below: DRAM >= ~85% of its SOL at ratio ~1 — the byte floor is already held at the traffic level; re-price the paper's band down and look at byte reduction (wider effective BN), not scheduling
decide-above: ratio >= ~2 — slot-tile re-read amplification confirmed at DRAM; the lever is candidate 1 (fewer weight reads per pass: tile batching / persistent slabs), its gain priced from the measured ratio x stack bytes
